You want a standalone TS library that exposes React 19.2.3 components using Puck + Tailwind 4, installable in any React app. Here is a concrete, production-grade pattern.

## High-level architecture

- Build a **regular npm package** (not a separate app) that:
  - Declares `react`, `react-dom`, and `tailwindcss` as **peerDependencies** pinned to React 19.2.3 and Tailwind 4 ranges.[1][2][3][4]
  - Bundles:
    - Your UI components
    - Puck’s editor and renderer wrappers
    - A small Tailwind 4 preset / CSS layer
- Consumers:
  - Install the package and peer deps.
  - Import components and your Tailwind CSS layer in their app entry.

This keeps React/Tailwind single-instanced and avoids style conflicts.

## Package setup (TS + bundler)

Use TS + a modern bundler like **tsup**, **Vite library mode**, or **Rollup**. Example with `tsup`:

**package.json**

```jsonc
{
  "name": "@your-scope/puck-ui",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "main": "dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "dist/index.mjs",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs",
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
    }
  },
  "peerDependencies": {
    "react": "^19.2.3",
    "react-dom": "^19.2.3",
    "tailwindcss": "^4.0.0",
    "@measured/puck": "^0.18.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "^5.7.0",
    "tsup": "^8.0.0",
    "tailwindcss": "^4.0.0"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsup src/index.tsx --dts --format cjs,esm --sourcemap"
  }
}
```

**tsconfig.json**

```jsonc
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "declaration": true,
    "emitDeclarationOnly": false,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "moduleResolution": "Node",
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "baseUrl": "src",
    "paths": {
      "@puck-ui/*": ["./*"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
```

`tsup` works well for React libraries and will keep your bundle small.[2][5][6][1]

## Tailwind 4 in a library

Tailwind 4 uses a **CSS-first** approach with its new engine; consumers still need Tailwind configured in the host app.[3][4][7][8]

Recommended pattern:

- Your library ships:
  - A **CSS file** that defines the design tokens / layer for your components.
  - Optionally a **Tailwind preset** (host app can extend their config with it).

**src/styles/tailwind.css**

```css
@layer base {
  :root {
    --puck-ui-bg: theme(colors.zinc.950);
    --puck-ui-fg: theme(colors.zinc.50);
  }
}

@layer components {
  .puck-ui-card {
    @apply rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-900/70 p-4;
  }

  .puck-ui-button-primary {
    @apply inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg
      bg-indigo-500 px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-white
      hover:bg-indigo-400 active:bg-indigo-500/90;
  }
}
```

Consumers should:

```ts
// e.g. in src/main.tsx or app entry
import '@your-scope/puck-ui/styles.css'; // re-export of tailwind.css from your package
```

And ensure their Tailwind 4 scanning picks your components by including `node_modules/@your-scope/puck-ui/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}` in `content` if Tailwind 4 config still uses content globs in their setup.[4][7][8][3]

## Puck integration in the library

Puck is “just React components”, so you can wrap the editor and renderer in typed components.[5][6][9]

**src/puckConfig.tsx**

```tsx
import type { Config } from '@measured/puck';
import { Puck } from '@measured/puck';
import '@measured/puck/puck.css';

const config: Config = {
  components: {
    HeadingBlock: {
      fields: {
        children: { type: 'text' }
      },
      render: ({ children }) => (
        <h1 className="puck-ui-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-white">
          {children}
        </h1>
      )
    },
    Card: {
      fields: {
        title: { type: 'text' },
        body: { type: 'textarea' }
      },
      render: ({ title, body }) => (
        <div className="puck-ui-card">
          <h2 className="mb-2 text-lg font-medium text-white">{title}</h2>
          <p className="text-sm text-zinc-300">{body}</p>
        </div>
      )
    }
  }
};

export type PuckConfig = typeof config;
export { config };
```

**src/Editor.tsx**

```tsx
import * as React from 'react';
import { Puck } from '@measured/puck';
import type { PuckData } from '@measured/puck';
import { config } from './puckConfig';

export type PuckEditorProps = {
   PuckData;
  onPublish: ( PuckData) => void | Promise<void>;
};

export const PuckEditor: React.FC<PuckEditorProps> = ({ data, onPublish }) => {
  return <Puck config={config} data={data} onPublish={onPublish} />;
};
```

**src/Renderer.tsx**

```tsx
import * as React from 'react';
import { Render, type PuckData } from '@measured/puck';
import { config } from './puckConfig';

export type PuckRendererProps = {
   PuckData;
};

export const PuckRenderer: React.FC<PuckRendererProps> = ({ data }) => {
  return <Render config={config} data={data} />;
};
```

**src/index.ts**

```ts
export * from './Editor';
export * from './Renderer';
export * from './puckConfig';
export * from './components'; // your extra primitives if any
import './styles.css';
```

This gives consumers a simple API: they just use `<PuckEditor>` and `<PuckRenderer>` wired to their own persistence.

## Consuming from any React app

In a host React 19 project (Next.js, Vite, CRA-like):

1. Install:

```bash
pnpm add @your-scope/puck-ui
pnpm add -D tailwindcss@^4.0.0
```

2. Ensure React 19.2.3 and Tailwind 4 match peer ranges.[1][2][3][4]
3. Import CSS once at the top-level entry:

```ts
import '@your-scope/puck-ui/styles.css';
```

4. Use components:

```tsx
import { PuckEditor, PuckRenderer } from '@your-scope/puck-ui';

const initialData = { /* PuckData from DB or blank */ };

export function Page() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(initialData);

  return (
    <>
      <PuckEditor
        data={data}
        onPublish={(d) => {
          setData(d);
          // persist to API / DB here
        }}
      />
      <PuckRenderer data={data} />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Module design tips for you as a TS expert

- Keep **React 19 APIs** (hooks, Server Components constraints) in mind but avoid importing `react-dom/client` from the library; let the host app own rendering.[10][11][12][2]
- Prefer **named exports** from your package; avoid default export to keep tree-shaking clean.
- Treat Puck and Tailwind as **peerDependencies** when you want the host to control versions; otherwise keep Puck as a dependency but still peer React/Tailwind.[6][9][5]
- Add **strict type exports** for Puck data/config so host apps can extend your config without losing types.

